Rerun (rerun.io) bridge: stream PINNeAPPle results into the Rerun viewer, live or into an ``.rrd`` file.

Rerun is a time-aware multimodal viewer: 3-D scenes, point clouds, meshes, time series, text and graphs on shared
timelines, scrubbable after the fact. That fits digital twins and simulations, where a field, a sensor reading, the
active model and a diagnosis all change with time. This bridge logs:

* :meth:`RerunLogger.scene`: a ``studio.Scene`` (surfaces coloured by a field, streamlines);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.points` / :meth:`RerunLogger.mesh`: raw point clouds and triangle meshes;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.molecule`: a molecule (and its metal slab) from :mod:`..molecular`, frame by frame;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.scalars`: time series (sensor values, errors, weights of an ensemble);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.ensemble_run`: a :class:`~pinneapple_physics.ensemble.EnsembleRun` (weights, errors, active
  model per case);
* :meth:`RerunLogger.graph`: a knowledge graph or a proof graph as a graph view, nodes coloured by status;
* :meth:`RerunLogger.text`: a Markdown report (physics audit, proof report).

    from pinneapple_tools.visualization.integrations.rerun_bridge import RerunLogger
    log = RerunLogger("pinneapple", path="run.rrd")          # or spawn=True for a live viewer
    log.molecule(mol, frames, times, slab=slab)
    log.close()                                              # then: rerun run.rrd

Needs ``pip install rerun-sdk``.

Molecular visualisation: proteins and molecules, alone or adsorbed on metal surfaces, rendered as stills, MP4
trajectories, 3-D scenes (glTF for the PINNeAPPle web viewer) and MD dashboards.

The visual side of a protein-on-metal MD pipeline (the kind RoyalMD Gold runs with OpenMM), with nothing of an MD
engine in it: anything that gives atom positions can be shown (a PDB file, an OpenMM or mdtraj trajectory, the
torch MD of ``pinneapple_simulation``).

* :class:`Molecule`: atoms read from PDB (multi-MODEL files give the frames), element and residue chemistry,
  CA backbone trace, per-residue class (hydrophobic, polar, positive, negative, special);
* :func:`fcc_slab`: an fcc metal slab (Au, Ag, Cu, Pt, Pd, Ni, Al, Pb) with a (111), (100) or (110) top facet, sized
  in whole lattice repeats so a periodic box does not strain it; :func:`slab_for_footprint` sizes it from a
  molecule's footprint plus padding;
* :func:`orient_on_surface`: principal axes of the molecule, the thinnest direction along the surface normal (the
  broadest face down), an optional active site turned away from the surface, and a set gap above the slab;
* :func:`render`: a still with pyvista (off-screen): atoms as spheres coloured by residue class, element, chain or
  height, a backbone tube, the slab with a metallic material, three-point lighting;
* :func:`render_frames` / :func:`encode_mp4`: a trajectory as PNG frames and an H.264 MP4 (imageio-ffmpeg), and
  :func:`pinneapple_tools.visualization.studio.particles.compose_video` adds live charts next to the render;
* :func:`md_dashboard`: energy, temperature, RMSD, height above the surface and contacts over time;
* :func:`to_glb`: the scene as glTF (one mesh per material) for the three.js viewer;
* :class:`StepLog`: readable, optionally coloured step-by-step console log of a pipeline.

functions.doc:
``read_openfoam`` for a case or a ``.foam`` file, ``read_vtk`` for a VTK file: one name for both.

Extra keyword arguments (``time``, ``fields``, ``patches=True``, ``all_times=True``) go to the OpenFOAM reader;
``patches=True`` returns the boundary instead of the volume.

classes.doc:
Polylines (e.g. streamlines), each with optional per-point values (e.g. speed). ``cmap``: "jet" or the
diverging "coolwarm" / "bwr"; ``color_range`` (default: 1st..99th percentile, symmetric for a diverging map);
``alpha`` < 1 and ``radius`` (tube radius; default from the scene size) for soft translucent wisps.
